---
import MaterialsTable from '@/components/ui/MaterialsTable.astro';
import Todo from '@/components/ui/Todo.astro';
import TorsionSpring from '@/components/diagrams/TorsionSpring.astro';
import { torsion } from '@/data/products';
import ProductLayout from '@/layouts/ProductLayout.astro';
---

<ProductLayout product={torsion}>
  <Fragment slot="about">
    <p>
      A torsion spring is a close-wound helix whose <strong>legs</strong> are rotated around the
      coil axis. It stores torque, not a push or a pull. That is why the drawing must give a free
      angle and a wind direction, not only a free length.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is the spring we make most of. Wire is {torsion.specs[0]?.value} from hard-drawn spring
      steel, music wire, stainless 302 / 304 / 316 or oil-tempered wire. Legs are bent to the
      drawing — straight, hooked or offset.
    </p>
    <p>
      Load the spring so the coils <strong>wind tighter</strong>. The inside diameter shrinks and
      the body grows a little along the axis. Put a shaft or mandrel through the ID; a good starting
      fit is an arbor of about 90 percent of the smallest ID at full deflection. Fatigue cracks
      usually start where the leg leaves the body, so a generous bend radius there is worth more
      than an extra coil.
    </p>
    <p>
      We do not publish a standard catalogue. Every torsion spring is a customer part: your wire,
      your ID, your legs, your angle.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="types">
    <div class="grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <figure class="type-fig">
        <div class="bp-grid type-draw">
          <TorsionSpring decorative />
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          <strong>Single-body torsion.</strong> One helix, two legs. Specify free angle and wind.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Leg forms we are asked for: straight, a simple hook, a 90-degree bend, or an offset that
          clears a boss. We will form what is on the drawing.
        </p>
        <Todo text="list the leg forms you regularly tool for." />
        <p>
          Wind is left-hand or right-hand. If the spring sits over a shaft that is already in the
          assembly, the wind is fixed by that layout — send a photo of the fit if you are unsure.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <MaterialsTable slot="materials" />

  <Fragment slot="tips">
    <p>
      Give us the <strong>inside diameter</strong>, not only the outside. The ID has to clear the
      shaft at full wind-up. If you only have the old spring, measure the wire with a micrometer if
      you can; a vernier on the OD is the next best thing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Torque at a working angle is useful but not required. Quantity and material (or “it lives
      outdoors / next to a switch”) are enough to start a quote.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="applications">
    <p>
      Typical work from our unit: <strong>switchgear and MCB mechanisms</strong>, small automotive
      assemblies, furniture recline and fold hinges, and instrumentation. The same layout turns up
      on appliance lids, clips and stationery — anywhere a light wire has to return a lever.
    </p>
    <p>
      We do not make rolling-shutter counterbalance springs or heavy torsion bars. Those need wire
      thicker than 2.50 mm.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two-wheeler stands and kick-starts are a common torsion application in Pune. Whether we
      already make those parts for you is still to confirm.
    </p>
    <Todo text="confirm two-wheeler torsion work before we name it as a regular product." />
  </Fragment>
</ProductLayout>

<style>
  .type-fig {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .type-draw {
    padding: 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-grid);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    color: var(--color-navy-900);
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-top: 10px;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
</style>
